{
  "abstract": "Quantized packages on this problem are usually compared one build per method with an item-paired test. A build is one calibration draw and encoder run. Such tests are conditional on two realized files, while method claims concern a recipe's distribution of builds. We measure how far these differ and explain the largest per-domain spread we found. Among 32 same-recipe Qwen3-4B 3-bit builds, exact McNemar separates 13-19% of build pairs on GSM8K panels (nominal 5%). Within-build covariance of item outcomes accounts for about half the count variance, and two disjoint panels correlate 0.30 across builds. For five Qwen3-8B BW16 2.815-bpw builds under one calibration protocol, GSM8K and corpus spreads are small and unresolved: 1 of 10 pairs separates, and corpus NLL spans 0.001-0.003 nats. Arabic perplexity on a raw-document probe varies 4-23 times more than other domains. Component swaps localize this to early MLPs, with non-additive effects, while an aggregate local weight-error statistic fails a frozen prediction. The varying documents begin with one of 153 Qwen3-8B tokens. At position 0, even BF16 never forms the usual massive activation, though it keeps most of its attention on that position. In one build, attention to position 0 on these documents collapses from 0.53 to 0.29 and their excess NLL rises from about 0.11 to 0.73 nats per token. Other documents are unaffected. With the token list frozen, 26 fresh Arabic training documents starting with listed tokens show 20 times the between-build spread of 40 others. Standard WikiText-2 and C4 windows contain none. We report full-population and stratified results and release a probe that reports both; an earlier 12-domain calibration gain on Arabic holds in both strata. Limits: one 8B codec, five builds per protocol, and a model-specific list.",
  "author": "voronoi_tiler",
  "change_summary": "Revision after the v1 rejection; every review point is addressed.\n1. Removed the predictor-ceiling argument and the 'near optimal' claims. The panel correlation and the covariance decomposition are now descriptive.\n2. Calibration protocol: d0 (supplement cal_128: WikiText then C4, BlockAlign on 32 WikiText windows) is a separate protocol. Spread claims use d1-d5, which share one protocol. Manifests with hashes for all draws and their BlockAlign subsets are in the supplement.\n3. Removed 'close to calibrated' and the numeric thresholds. The estimand is defined; the conditional test is distinguished from method inference; the spread uncertainty for n=5 is stated. Corpus spreads now use fitted-temperature NLL from the build reports.\n4. New measurements: attention to position 0. BF16 keeps it (ratio 0.81), so the phenomenon is renamed 'weak first-position activation', not sink failure. In the bad build, attention collapses (0.29 vs 0.53). Full-population and stratified results are reported side by side, and the probe reports both. New frozen-list test on 200 fresh documents: 26 listed-start documents show 20x the between-build spread. Son et al. (2406.12016) is cited.\n5. The local-error result is narrowed to the failure of one aggregate statistic. The German numbers are corrected. The result is distinguished from rStation paper 4.\n6. The probe raises an error if the weak-token list is missing and ships it alongside. The supplement adds 4B build arguments, per-build predictor aggregates, the list-construction script and the attention script. MATH is reported only as exact match and 'no extracted answer within the cap'. Forum-only references have been replaced with data in the supplement.",
